<rml>
<head>
	<title>textured text</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="/folio/folio.rcss"/>
	<style>
		/*
			Text filled with a pattern rather than a flat colour.

			font-effect: fill-image draws the glyph a second time at the same coverage with a
			texture multiplied in, as a front layer. That is why an outline or a glow on the same
			text stays flat: only the fill layer is textured.

			The pattern is anchored to the screen, not to the text, so a scanline stays put while
			text moves under it - which is what a scanline is for, and worth knowing before using
			it for something that should travel with the words.

			It names a file, not a sprite. A font effect is built while the stylesheet parses and
			has no route to a spritesheet.
		*/
		body
		{
			font-family: Inter;
			font-size: 54dp;
			color: #f0c674;
			background-color: #16161c;
			padding: 24dp;
			width: 100%;
			height: 100%;
			box-sizing: border-box;
		}

		.label { font-size: 13dp; color: #8a8a99; margin-bottom: 2dp; font-effect: none; }
		.row { margin-bottom: 14dp; }

		.plain   { }
		.striped { font-effect: fill-image("/UI/art/scanlines.png" 6dp); }
		.fine    { font-effect: fill-image("/UI/art/scanlines.png" 3dp); }
		.tinted  { color: #4aa3df; font-effect: fill-image("/UI/art/scanlines.png" 6dp); }
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<div class="label">no fill</div>
	<div class="row plain">Handgloves</div>

	<div class="label">fill-image, 6dp tile</div>
	<div class="row striped">Handgloves</div>

	<div class="label">fill-image, 3dp tile</div>
	<div class="row fine">Handgloves</div>

	<div class="label">the flat colour still tints what shows through</div>
	<div class="row tinted">Handgloves</div>
</body>
</rml>
